   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 6629950,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 1269703,
    "poster_id": 213285,
    "post_rendered": "In the beginning there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" > integers in a row on the blackboard. Kain and Abel then play the following game: A <i>move</i> consists in Kain choosing a chain of consecutive numbers; the length of the chain can be any of the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/d/8ad1909becde34012ebb60cd9ce63905a426b267.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1,2,\\dots,100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"93\" height=\"16\" > and in particular it is allowed that Kain only chooses a single number. After Kain has chosen his chain of numbers, Abel has to decide whether he wants to add <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > to each of the chosen numbers or instead subtract <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"8\" height=\"12\" > from of the numbers. After that the next move begins, and so on.<br>\n<br>\nIf there are at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/1/73119f04d6ae5f6eb4afc8c9c946a5744a11be50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$98$\"  width=\"17\" height=\"12\" > numbers on the blackboard that are divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > after a move, then Kain has won.<br>\n<br>\nProve that Kain can force a win in a finite number of moves.",
    "post_canonical": "In the beginning there are $100$ integers in a row on the blackboard. Kain and Abel then play the following game: A [i]move[/i] consists in Kain choosing a chain of consecutive numbers; the length of the chain can be any of the numbers $1,2,\\dots,100$ and in particular it is allowed that Kain only chooses a single number. After Kain has chosen his chain of numbers, Abel has to decide whether he wants to add $1$ to each of the chosen numbers or instead subtract $1$ from of the numbers. After that the next move begins, and so on.\n\nIf there are at least $98$ numbers on the blackboard that are divisible by $4$ after a move, then Kain has won.\n\nProve that Kain can force a win in a finite number of moves.",
    "username": "Kezer",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_213285.jpg?t=1498485447"
   }
